 need to be met for people to ascribe intentionality.  We agreed that there may be several noteworthy deviations from this complex set of conditions\emdash e.g., when adults ascribe intentionality to children or animals, or when children ascribe intentionality to (e.g., the desire-intention distinction appears to emerge around age 4).  \par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab The question for this week is how people make judgments about intentionality for ongoing behaviors.  This large question breaks down in a series of smaller questions:\par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab How do people parse the stream of behavior that other people produce (and how do children learn to do the same?)\par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab How do people conceptualize the units of behavior that they parse?  In particular, how do they distinguish between intentional behaviors and unintentional behaviors?\par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab If a behavior is being judged as intentional, how do people infer the intention\emdash or, more important, the goal behind it?\par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab It appears as if these tasks follow a temporal sequence: parsing --> judging intentionality --> inferring specific intentions/goals.  However, this assumption quickly runs into difficulties.  For example, to parse an action of \ldblquote phoning somebody\rdblquote  the perceiver needs to have the concept of \ldblquote phoning\rdblquote  and needs to ascribe the intention of phoning to a particular sequences of movements. So an infant (or a Martian) who does not have the concept of phoning will not be able to parse that action.  But can the infant (or Martian) at least parse that the agent does {\b something} that is distinguished from other things without knowing {\b what} the agent is doing?\par \pard \widctlpar \par \pard \widctlpar \tab The answer to this question depends on how we interpret  Newtson\rquote s work (exemplified in Newtson & Enquist, 1976).  He shows quite convincingly that adults can parse a stream of behavior into \ldblquote meaningful units,\rdblquote  that the breakpoints between these units are processed attentively, and that they are critical to the interpretation of the entire stream.  However, what constitutes those breakpoints?  Change of moveme